<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kasugayama Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kasugayama Castle was a mountain castle near modern Jōetsu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata-prefecture">Niigata Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a> in the 16th century. From it the Nagao and Uesugi lords controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echigo-province">Echigo Province</a>. After the Uesugi were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> in 1598 it was abandoned in the early 17th century for a new castle on the plain, and its earthworks are now a national historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kasugayama-castle.jpg" alt="Kasugayama Castle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 柴錬アワー · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kasaugayama_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
